         |  24 ++++\n internal/servecmd/connect_test.go      | 201 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++\n internal/servecmd/serve.go             |  83 +++++++++--\n 18 files changed, 1046 insertions(+), 25 deletions(-)\n\ndiff --git a/README.md b/README.md\nindex 8b74fc8..5ce1b29 100644\n--- a/README.md\n+++ b/README.md\n@@ -41,7 +41,7 @@ See [Configuration](docs/proposal.md#configuration) in the proposal for the full\n \n ## Getting started\n \n-Install the App, run `ard init`, commit and merge the tree, run `ard setup`, fix any manual rows it reports, try `ard serve --dry-run`, then go live. See [Getting started](docs/site/getting-started.md).\n+Pick credentials: a GitHub App (see Getting started), a personal access token in `ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` (a fine-grained, single-repo token with an expiry is best), or nothing, in which case `ard serve` uses a logged-in `gh` CLI's token for the session. `ard serve` checks the credentials against the repository before it starts any loop. Then install the App if you use one, run `ard init`, commit and merge the tree, run `ard setup`, fix any manual rows it reports, try `ard serve --dry-run`, then go live. See [Getting started](docs/site/getting-started.md).\n \n ## Development\n \ndiff --git a/docs/proposal.md b/docs/proposal.md\nindex 9f7863d..8745d7e 100644\n--- a/docs/proposal.md\n+++ b/docs/proposal.md\n@@ -522,7 +522,15 @@ What `true` and `false` mean per executor, as the code does today: the host exec\n \n Agents with push access read text that strangers can write, so the controls sit in code and permissions, not in prompts.\n \n-- **Identity: a GitHub App, not a PAT.** Installation tokens expire hourly and are scoped to contents, issues and pull requests (read and write), plus checks (read and write) and commit statuses (read). The merger reads check runs (checks: read), commit statuses (commit statuses: read) and the branch rules (metadata). The merger needs read to see required checks; the owner kept write so ard can later publish its own check runs, but nothing in Phase 1 writes checks. Leaving out the `workflows` permission makes GitHub itself reject any push that edits `.github/workflows/`, a second backstop behind a guard that already refuses all of `.github/`, and it also keeps the bot from editing the bot-commit audit. The scratch-repo run (#85) confirmed that GitHub rejects such a push (\"refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow ... without `workflows` permission\"), and that these work with exactly this permission set: label writes, PR create, review and mark-ready, pinning the status issue, and timeline and collaborator-permission reads. A personal token (`ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN`) is accepted only for local `--dry-run` development, and the client is then made read-only so no write can reach GitHub.\n+- **Identity: a GitHub App or a PAT.** Installation tokens expire hourly and are scoped to contents, issues and pull requests (read and write), plus checks (read and write) and commit statuses (read). The merger reads check runs (checks: read), commit statuses (commit statuses: read) and the branch rules (metadata). The merger needs read to see required checks; the owner kept write so ard can later publish its own check runs, but nothing in Phase 1 writes checks. Leaving out the `workflows` permission makes GitHub itself reject any push that edits `.github/workflows/`, a second backstop behind a guard that already refuses all of `.github/`, and it also keeps the bot from editing the bot-commit audit. The scratch-repo run (#85) confirmed that GitHub rejects such a push (\"refusing to allow a GitHub App to create or update workflow ... without `workflows` permission\"), and that these work with exactly this permission set: label writes, PR create, review and mark-ready, pinning the status issue, and timeline and collaborator-permission reads. ard works with either a GitHub App or a personal access token (`ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN`), live or dry-run. With neither set, `ard serve` falls back to the `gh` CLI: absent, it exits listing both ways to configure auth; logged out, it prints `gh auth login --hostname github.com --git-protocol https --scopes repo` and exits; logged in, it says it will run `gh auth token`, reads the token once and uses it as a PAT for the session. That token is held in memory only: never written to disk, logged, passed to agents or put in a job's environment. Before any loop starts, `ard serve` checks the credentials against the target repo (`GET /repos/{owner}/{repo}` plus the permissions ard needs: contents, issues and pull requests write, checks and statuses read, for an App from the installation, for a token from the repo's `permissions` and a classic PAT's `X-OAuth-Scopes`) and exits non-zero with one line naming what is missing. It logs `github.connect` once with the auth kind, login, repo and permissions found. Under `--dry-run` only read access is required.\n+\n+  What a PAT changes:\n+  - **Identity.** Issues, comments, commits on `worker/` branches and PRs are made as the token's user, not the App's bot.\n+  - **Self-review.** ard posts every review as a `COMMENT` and gates on its own `.ard/<issue#>/` records, so nothing depends on GitHub approving a PR. The merger's protected-path check ignores reviews by ard's own login, which under a PAT is the token's user: that user's own approval does not count, and a PAT setup needs protected-path approvals from a different write-access user.\n+  - **Bot-commit audit.** `.github/workflows/bot-commits.yml` keys on the App's identity (`ARD_BOT_LOGIN`). With a PAT, main-lane commits under `.ard/<issue#>/` are the user's and the audit cannot tell them from the user's own commits. Setting `ARD_BOT_LOGIN` to the user would flag every human commit, so leave it unset.\n+  - **Workflows backstop.** An App without `workflows` cannot push `.github/workflows/`. A classic `repo`-scoped PAT can (without the `workflow` scope it cannot), and a fine-grained PAT only if granted. The code guard still refuses `.github/`. Use a fine-grained PAT scoped to the one repo, without workflows.\n+  - **Expiry and scope.** A PAT does not expire hourly. Use a fine-grained, single-repo token with an expiry.\n+  - A fine-grained PAT lists no permissions, so the startup check verifies contents write from the repo and issues and pull request access with one read each; checks and statuses read are not probed.\n - **The GitHub client never follows redirects.** A redirect can turn a write into a silent GET, replay it into another repository, or carry the token to another host. An issue that redirects to another repository (transferred) reads as not found. A repository that redirects (renamed or transferred) is a hard, non-transient error naming the new name when GitHub reveals it. ard does not follow a rename; point it at the new name. A deleted issue (410 Gone) also reads as not found.\n - **Agents hold no GitHub token.** The executor does. The agent's environment has only its model credential: an API key or a subscription OAuth token. The local executor does not enforce egress (see `network` under Configuration; the chart's NetworkPolicy comment is #296). In Kubernetes (#54) the jobs namespace starts from a default-deny NetworkPolicy. Every job pod may reach cluster DNS, and GitHub's and the model API's CIDRs on TCP 443 (`jobs.githubCIDRs`, `jobs.modelAPICIDRs` in the chart; `pre` fetches from GitHub); a pod not labelled `network: false` may also reach the internet minus private ranges and 169.254.169.254 (the #276 posture). NetworkPolicy is per pod, so a `network: false` agent still reaches the GitHub and model API CIDRs. `pre` holds only a read-only per-job token revoked after it ends.\n - **Isolated agent pod.** The agent container holds only its model credential. It comes from `ard serve`'s own environment (`ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` or `CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN`, the only two names it forwards) into each job's Secret; in the chart, the controller container gets that one variable from the Secret `modelCredential.secretName` (created by hand or through External Secrets), and a live k8s install without it is refused ([#434](https://github.com/albertcbrown/ard/issues/434)). `pre` and `post` run as a different UID. A ValidatingAdmissionPolicy enforces `shareProcessNamespace: false` and refuses the App Secret, hostPath, a Secret mount in the agent container and an agent `runAsUser` equal to the sidecars'.\ndiff --git a/docs/runbooks/onboard-repo.md b/docs/runbooks/onboard-repo.md\nindex 887d2a8..1ce4d2e 100644\n--- a/docs/runbooks/onboard-repo.md\n+++ b/docs/runbooks/onboard-repo.md\n@@ -84,6 +84,15 @@ Dry-run keeps its own state in `~/.ard/state-dryrun/<owner>/<repo>/` and writes\n intended effects to `effects.jsonl`. Soak and read the ledger, then start\n `ard serve` without the flag.\n \n+ard serve checks GitHub at startup and exits with one line if the repository\n+is unreachable or a permission is missing. Instead of a GitHub App you can set\n+`ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` (a fine-grained, single-repo token with an expiry), or set\n+nothing and log in with `gh auth login --hostname github.com --git-protocol\n+https --scopes repo`: ard then uses the `gh` token for the session, in memory\n+only. With a token, ard acts as the token's user, so protected-path PRs need\n+approval from a different write-access user, and the bot-commit audit cannot\n+tell ard's main-lane commits from yours.\n+\n In the cluster, add an entry for the repository to the instances list in the\n deployment repository (check the key name in that repository's docs). Live agent\n jobs in the cluster need #54; until it lands, run the cluster instance in\ndiff --git a/docs/site/getting-started.md b/docs/site/getting-started.md\nindex 81c2107..806815d 100644\n--- a/docs/site/getting-started.md\n+++ b/docs/site/getting-started.md\n@@ -4,7 +4,7 @@ This takes a repository from nothing to a first triaged issue. Run the `ard` com\n \n ## 1. Create and install the GitHub App\n \n-ard acts as a GitHub App, never as a personal token. The quickest way to create one with the right permissions is the manifest flow:\n+ard acts as a GitHub App, or as a personal access token (`ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN`), or, when neither is set, as the token of a logged-in `gh` CLI (kept in memory for the session). A token acts as its user: protected-path PRs then need approval from a different write-access user, and the bot-commit audit cannot tell ard's commits from yours. Use a fine-grained, single-repo token with an expiry. `ard serve` checks the credentials against the repository before any loop starts. The App is the default path; the quickest way to create one with the right permissions is the manifest flow:\n \n ```sh\n ard setup --create-gh-app --dry-run   # preview the manifest and URL; creates nothing\ndiff --git a/docs/site/operations/gate-2-runbook.md b/docs/site/operations/gate-2-runbook.md\nindex 1eaff10..739a121 100644\n--- a/docs/site/operations/gate-2-runbook.md\n+++ b/docs/site/operations/gate-2-runbook.md\n@@ -55,8 +55,8 @@ CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN=$(op read \"op://Private/claude setup-token/credential\")\n export CLAUDE_CODE_OAUTH_TOKEN\n ```\n \n-`ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` must stay unset: a personal token makes the client\n-read-only and is refused outside `--dry-run`.\n+`ARD_GITHUB_TOKEN` must stay unset for this run: setting it together with the\n+App variables is an error, and a token would act as your user, not the App.\n \n ## Stage A: smoke run on ard-scratch\n \ndiff --git a/internal/configcmd/config.go b/internal/configcmd/config.go\nindex 38b2219..ba77049 100644\n--- a/internal/configcmd/config.go\n+++ b/internal/configcmd/config.go\n@@ -14,6 +14,7 @@ import (\n \t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/config\"\n \t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/executor\"\n \t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/executor/local\"\n+\t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/github\"\n \t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/internal/gitx\"\n \t\"github.com/albertcbrown/ard/